{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553494-how-frequent-should-enemies-appear-from-the-enemy-flaw","id":1553494,"title":"How frequent should Enemies appear from the Enemy Flaw?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-27T17:27:07-07:00","id":1553494,"title":"How frequent should Enemies appear from the Enemy Flaw?","content":"I think almost all the games in all editions have the Enemy flaw. It's a flaw which you can take from 1-5 points, with one point being a single person near your own character's power level and five points being some of the most powerful characters in that game. The other points don't get described, but I generally see three points as defined as having the equivalent power of your coterie, pack, motley, etc., and you can figure out the other levels accordingly.<br /> <br /> However, what is the frequency that such enemies should happen to appear or intervene in game? Not having them show up at all in the chronicle is clearly wrong. The PC has freebies for nothing, and most players actually want their enemies to appear at some point in time. Likewise, having such enemies be omnipresent in the chronicle also seems wrong. That just hijacks the entire chronicle and likely interrupts the PC too much so he is unable to do anything else except deal with their enemy.<br /> <br /> So it is all about finding that sweet spot. I don't think the flaw should be something that is addressed and resolved in one single story. I think it's something that should keep making an appearance UNTIL the player finally decides to buy the Flaw off along with a proper resolution. Ideally, the ST should also present it in a way that until the end, it really bedevils that one PC and not the entire player character group (after all, they didn't get any freebies from it). However, how much time should it show up in game? What do people think?<br /> <br /> Is this something that might take up 10% of the total time spent in game on average? Less? More? Do you handle it in some other way than a time percentage of game sessions/time at table? If so, what is the general metric or guideline that you strive for?<br /> <br /> I understand there is no &quot;one&quot; correct way to handle this. I'm just interested in seeing how different STs approach this.<br /> <br /> BTW, I don't think the Enemy flaw necessarily means the enemy wants to kill that character, or at least that every time the enemy &quot;strikes&quot; it is to kill. I think that creates more problems than what it's worth. Such enemies need to be able to thwart, hamper, or harm the PC in other ways for them to become good long term antagonists. J Jonah Jameson is just as good an enemy as is Norman Osborn for Spider-Man.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-27T19:41:39-07:00","id":1553502,"title":"","content":"In my current Mage chronicle my wife is playing with this Flaw. In this case, the Enemy is actually the husband of her character, and he's a loving husband.<br /> <br /> So loving he's completely against her character endangering himself, so whenever the Enemy shows up his goal is simply to drag the PC back home to safety. The Enemy in question is also a Werewolf.<br /> <br /> What I did was to establish a base 10% chance per session for the Enemy to &quot;show up&quot; somehow, but once he does he'll be a nuisance for the rest of the story, unless the group arranges to get him off track somehow.<br /> <br /> For now it has worked and I'm happy with the characters and dynamic, but I have to say it mostly reinforced my dislike of this Flaw as a mechanic. Sincerely? It does just hijack the narrative, even if just for a while. If a player wants an &quot;enemy&quot;, it's easier for me to just include this enemy in the story and bring them whenever it's organically relevant, than be forced to find a balance that justify a set number of Freebies and then keep a somewhat static <i>status quo</i> until the Flaw is bought back.<br /> <br /> And I'm not even talking about how hard it is to balance power and the level of interference of the Enemy, especially with how vague the two described levels are so described. I'd much prefer a CoD's Ally type of thing, where particular mechanical effects are given to represent the actual trait, and you're otherwise free to handle the story aspect as you see fit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T07:27:01-07:00","id":1553512,"title":"","content":"I say, let Enemies appear when it's organic for them to appear.<br /> <br /> Of course, the story needs to include them somehow. But the same can be said about any other trait - ideally there shouldn't be any loose thread, and I'm not going to be more or less obsesive about this just because the player it's gaining something out of it.<br /> <br /> As I see it, including the enemy on the chronicle it's my job, and if there's a chance I fail to do it - of course it is - it's not a basis to create a mechanic to punish the player <i>just in case</i>. If I miss I miss, life goes on, the show must continue, and so on - as long as people it's having fun that's all that matters. <hr /><br /> Merits and Flaws, as a system, it's a can of worms. The whole system it's a poorly designed popurry of unbalanced stuff that always was like blood on water for players wanting to gain stuff for nothing (or as cheap as possible). Everyone in this forum knows it. The authors know it, and that's why it's optional. <br /> <br /> I would recomend to not use Merit's &amp; Flaws if one doesn't like some characters being sligthly stronger than others, or getting &quot;free stuff&quot;. But, really, with how unbalanced WoD games are from the get go, I see it as the proberbial &quot;one more stripe to the tigger&quot;, and it ain't even a big one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T09:11:00-07:00","id":1553515,"title":"","content":"I don't have a problem with characters being stronger, I have a problem with a trait that puts the responsibility of making it work squarely in my shoulders.<br /> <br /> Bringing any aspect of the game to the story is my job, but traits in general (of any type) have rules for two reasons: to ease my work on implementing them, and to guide player expectation on how they'll play out. On one side, rules are there to guarantee we're mostly on the same page about how things will play out and ground player expectations, so they reliably know what to expect. On the other, to help me adjudicate things and prepare, so I deliver my story in a way that's sufficiently consistent.<br /> <br /> Traits are basically rules under the player's decisions, they're a way to give players a degree of control over the story. It's not just about making what they want, it's about making their input work in a reliable manner, one I can implement with some ease and consistency and they know what to expect.<br /> <br /> While the system has many problems with traits, and rules in general, that don't work so well, Enemy is a complete failure as game design. It makes this story element more complicated instead of simpler. It interferes with my story without giving any directions to ease the process. It either hijacks my story or take out from my player any real autonomy beyond making the vague suggestion of a character, without delivering any consistency or reliability.<br /> <br /> Enemy isn't a bad trait because it's potentially &quot;free points&quot;. It's a bad trait because we're literally better off just discussing the chronicle beforehand, it adds nothing to the process.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T12:06:33-07:00","id":1553519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553494-how-frequent-should-enemies-appear-from-the-enemy-flaw?p=1553512#post1553512\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I say, let Enemies appear when it's organic for them to appear.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think this really helps. It's seems tautological to me. It's another way of saying &quot;Enemies should appear whenever they should appear.&quot;<br /> <br /> In the context of an RPG, the group of PCs will interact with the setting according to the things they proactively do (we go into the dungeon) or inadvertently do (they kill a bandit, but don't realize it's not some random bandit but the lover of the Queen of Thieves who controls the Thieves Guild). The NPCs then act in response given their prior goals, objectives, and plans. They aren't necessarily interested in the specific PC or PCs unless they become an obstacle. The ST can adjudicate then what happens. Since it is generally the group of PCs who do this as a group, nobody is either left out or dragged in. That interplay can be &quot;organic&quot; because it is an interaction caused by their own choices and responses.<br /> <br /> I think the Enemy flaw is different because one person (or a group of enemies) are seeking to harm one specific PC. That's their objective (although they may have more goals, that is irrelevant to the flaw at hand). They have to be proactive about it because they aren't reacting to what the PCs are doing.<br /> <br /> &quot;Organically&quot; that could mean the Enemy is going to show up as often as they can. That can be easy to justify. However, I don't think that's going to be very fun for the PC. The player, typically, imagines that the Enemy will come into play every so often. Not that they are constantly fending that enemy off every game session or other high frequency. They want to be able to accomplish other goals and progress on whatever the main plot is. At a certain point, it is no longer fun, but becomes frustration instead even if it happens &quot;organically&quot;. You want to avoid that as an ST.<br /> <br /> So again it goes back to what is the &quot;sweet spot&quot;? You don't want the Enemy to show up too often, and you don't want it to show up too little. You also want the focus to remain on that one PC, and not have it interrupt the entire PC party or drag them into something they don't want to be involved in. (If another PC chooses to become involved, that's fine as it is their choice just like everything else in the game. However, I don't think the expectations should be that the flaws of one PC should become the flaws of all the other PCs).<br /> <br /> Of course, the ST can always ask the player in question about the frequency. However, I've found that in many cases the players themselves don't have any idea. They'll just tell you about wanting it to be fun or interesting, but not hijack from the game. So such talks are often not helpful. Only after you run the chronicle for a while will the player often constructive feedback; i.e. &quot;I was hoping to see my Enemy more often.&quot; or &quot;I can't seem to get anything else done, my Enemy is really hijacking every game session.&quot; That feedback is going to happen at some point, but it can't help you for some time.<br /> <br /> Again, I don't think there is &quot;one&quot; best answer. You can even start out one way (I'll try to include a mention of the Enemy 10% of the time), and it could turn into something else as the game continues (The other players seem to like dealing with Bob's Enemy. This is creating a lot of fun for them. Maybe I'll upgrade them to be a major opponent in the chronicle.) Something like that can happen &quot;organically&quot;, but it's not guaranteed.<br /> <br /> However, I do think it's useful to hear how other STs handle it. There might be an unexpected consensus. Or maybe there's a wide a wide variety of approaches, and hearing them may prove useful to other STs when thinking about how to implement this flaw. Having multiple ways to handle things gives more options to STs as not all Enemies act the same. You can try one way with one Enemy, and a second way with another. Or even change mid-streak if you see one approach doesn't seem to be working.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T12:32:12-07:00","id":1553520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't have a problem with characters being stronger, I have a problem with a trait that puts the responsibility of making it work squarely in my shoulders.<br /> <br /> Bringing any aspect of the game to the story is my job, but traits in general (of any type) have rules for two reasons: to ease my work on implementing them, and to guide player expectation on how they'll play out. On one side, rules are there to guarantee we're mostly on the same page about how things will play out and ground player expectations, so they reliably know what to expect. On the other, to help me adjudicate things and prepare, so I deliver my story in a way that's sufficiently consistent.<br /> <br /> Traits are basically rules under the player's decisions, they're a way to give players a degree of control over the story. It's not just about making what they want, it's about making their input work in a reliable manner, one I can implement with some ease and consistency and they know what to expect.<br /> <br /> While the system has many problems with traits, and rules in general, that don't work so well, Enemy is a complete failure as game design. It makes this story element more complicated instead of simpler. It interferes with my story without giving any directions to ease the process. It either hijacks my story or take out from my player any real autonomy beyond making the vague suggestion of a character, without delivering any consistency or reliability.<br /> <br /> Enemy isn't a bad trait because it's potentially &quot;free points&quot;. It's a bad trait because we're literally better off just discussing the chronicle beforehand, it adds nothing to the process.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> mentioned something about the PC having freebies for nothing, so I tought important to consider that &quot;Merits and Flaws&quot; have a history of doing that, or being intended to do that by the players. That I don't think it's a big deal<br /> <br /> Now, on your objections: I'm not saying Enemy it's a good design, nor that it's adding anything to the process - quite the contrary - but I'm failing to see why this it's a <i>special </i>problem rather than just WoD being WoD.<br /> <br /> For instance, a Mentor also has to be taken in count as part of the story and the setting, also can be integrally built by discussing the chronicle beforehand, and often has to because WoD characters have to be introduced to their secret societies by someone - if you don't have a Mentor, it's still likely you <i>had</i> one. The indications about what a Mentor means, it's power, it's reach, are every bit as vague if not more than Enemy (it's even similar in that a weaker mentor that's more present it's supposed to cost more).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T12:56:15-07:00","id":1553521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553494-how-frequent-should-enemies-appear-from-the-enemy-flaw?p=1553519#post1553519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, I don't think there is &quot;one&quot; best answer. You can even start out one way (I'll try to include a mention of the Enemy 10% of the time), and it could turn into something else as the game continues (The other players seem to like dealing with Bob's Enemy. This is creating a lot of fun for them. Maybe I'll upgrade them to be a major opponent in the chronicle.) Something like that can happen &quot;organically&quot;, but it's not guaranteed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Leaving it to the dice, tables, and such, it's something I don't like to do, because I find that maybe the session I'm planning wasn't exactly conducent for the two 10 to suddenly appear. And I don't like to leave aside or change some idea I had just because dice said &quot;it's Enemy time&quot;<br /> <br /> It's just not my approach<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Organically&quot; that could mean the Enemy is going to show up as often as they can. That can be easy to justify. However, I don't think that's going to be very fun for the PC. </div> </div> </div>It could, but in the whole talking process that's the creation of an NPC, I would try to ensure the enemy isn't going to be a persona conducent to ruin the fun. It helps that I know how he's going to act because I'm going to act as them.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying this to trivialize the issue you're trying to push foward, it's just that I don't see how <b>this </b>one can be solved with maths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-28-2025, 04:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T14:20:15-07:00","id":1553525,"title":"","content":"While not necessarily directly helpful - as I think the structure of the Flaw makes it impossible to be helpful with it - my feelings on this is that this should be negotiated with the player during character creation. Basically, I turn &quot;frequency&quot; into part of the overall threat that dictates how many dots an Enemy is &quot;worth.&quot; So a fairly weak enemy that's a very regular presence in the game might be 2 or 3 dots instead of 1 to accommodate that.<br /> <br /> The big point is that it just gets negotiated out with the player and the group so everyone's on the same page and that when the enemy shows up it's not derailing, or disappointing when they don't.<br /> <br /> This is also helps avoid something like me having to accommodate an extremely potent enemy that's going to show up a lot. If I'm running VtM and you take the Prince of the city the game is set in as your enemy despite being a fresh starting level PC? The Prince is &quot;present&quot; sure, but the Prince has a lot more to worry about than some fresh neonate and can't afford to look weak by looking obsessed with one schmuck over their other duties. The Prince is not going to actively mess with the PC (and the rest of the coterie) with high frequency, but when the Prince does it's going to be a big deal. That just gets baked into the assumption once &quot;frequency&quot; is equated to the level of threat the enemy posses. Anything I would deem 6+ worth of enemy in both power and activity in the game gets excluded as an option for being too disruptive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-28T22:30:59-07:00","id":1553530,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553494-how-frequent-should-enemies-appear-from-the-enemy-flaw?p=1553520#post1553520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\">Black Fox</a> mentioned something about the PC having freebies for nothing, so I tought important to consider that &quot;Merits and Flaws&quot; have a history of doing that, or being intended to do that by the players. That I don't think it's a big deal</div> </div> </div> </div>For whatever it's worth, the fact the system in general fails at &quot;balance&quot; doesn't invalidate the search for it or an analysis of how much particular elements or aspects are worse offenders or not.<br /> <br /> So, while I mostly agree with you, the question does have its merit. This <b>can</b> affect the group's fun and Enemy is particularly problematic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553494-how-frequent-should-enemies-appear-from-the-enemy-flaw?p=1553520#post1553520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but I'm failing to see why this it's a <i>special </i>problem rather than just WoD being WoD.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bBecause most other traits are still capable at doing the job, including most other Merits and Flaws. They may not be <i>good</i> at it, but they do.<br /> <br /> <i>Mentor</i> is another very bad trait, so it isn't the best example for your argument. It just shows other bad designs exist among the long list of options.<br /> <br /> And even then: <i>Mentor</i> is meant to answer questions. It gives you advice and guidance. At the ST discretion it may help a <u><b>little</b></u> further by putting a good word for you. Anything more the very text suggests should come with strings attached or heavy consequences, and it says exactly what to do (ask for something in return or take out a dot of the Background).<br /> <br /> This is <i><b>extremely</b></i> more helpful than what Enemy does, even being one of the worse Background to work with. I know exactly what to do with it: offer advice when asked. The player knows exactly what to expect from it: they can ask for advice when they want. I have a few other tools to use it, but the player is directly told not to rely on that too much, and what the possible consequences are. So it eases my job, get us mostly on the same page, ground player expectation, it's reliable and consistent. I may use the character in my story in other ways, but as long as I deliver the minimum described we're all good and no one's frustrated.<br /> <br /> So I'd say <i>Mentor</i> itself is a good example of how even other bad designs can work far better than Enemy, so its level of problem is far from standard for the system.<br /> <br /> Just to be clear: I'm not against your take on how to handle the Flaw. I think it's as valid as anything else. If I correctly understood it, you basically make the Enemy as another NPC woven in your chronicle and &quot;call it a day&quot; as far as trait implementation goes, i.e. you just use it as your story dictates. I think you can still give useful feedback on what kind of criteria or advice you follow to build this character and its role, but is a perfectly valid take anyway. My only gripe here is with the notion that Enemy isn't meaningfully worse than anything else.\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553494-how-frequent-should-enemies-appear-from-the-enemy-flaw?p=1553525#post1553525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">my feelings on this is that this should be negotiated with the player during character creation. Basically, I turn &quot;frequency&quot; into part of the overall threat that dictates how many dots an Enemy is &quot;worth.&quot; So a fairly weak enemy that's a very regular presence in the game might be 2 or 3 dots instead of 1 to accommodate that.<br /> <br /> The big point is that it just gets negotiated out with the player and the group so everyone's on the same page and that when the enemy shows up it's not derailing, or disappointing when they don't.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an interesting take. Sharing the work, so to speak, so everyone's a part in the process and have a say in the final result. It solves some of the problems with expectation and consistency.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-29T08:03:09-07:00","id":1553536,"title":"","content":"Perhaps this goes a bit out of topic (or not?) but I've been thinking it could be useful as a tool to think a number of questions as a baseline to ask the player.<br /> <br /> I've been using standarized &quot;lists of questions&quot; as guidelines to generate my own NPC - and I don't see why the player couldn't use a similar list too on the Session 0 (well, I've seen players struggle with similar questions, so it's not really a <i>solution</i>, but maybe if we can think smart questions we can have a solid<i>-ish</i> starting point to get an intuitive grasp of how this is going to work out. At least more solid than the current &quot;do what thou wilt shall be the whole of the Law&quot;).<br /> <br /> *How much pressence do they have on the life of your character? it's an interesting question<br /> <br /> Some more tentative questions:<br /> <br /> *What kind of threat do they present to your character (social, physical,...)?<br /> <br /> *How do they threaten your character (do they use influence to thwart your plans, kill loved ones, ...)? , roughly what's their modus opperandi and resources<br /> <br /> *What are their goals (long term) and how does your character figure into them? - this one may be good to keep as an ST secret, YMMV, but maybe there's a more nuanced way to seek player input about this?<br /> <br /> *What does your character think about all of this?, what do they <i>want</i> to do about this enemy?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-29-2025, 11:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-30T13:48:15-07:00","id":1553557,"title":"","content":"Checking with the player when they take the flaw always seems the practical solution. They might overscale what they're imagining when selecting the flaw because 3 dots is more tempting than 2. As noted by others getting what they're thinking can help with integrating it into the story or just help you avoid something they're not interested in. And of course, that talk enables figuring out how frequently this enemy would show up. Like if they want this person to be a near constant threat they can be folded into the group currently planned as the big antagonist for a chronicle. You know like this group are the groups foes overall, but Koragg has it out for one member of the group specifically and will take any opportunity he can on the job to try and settle that issue.<br /> <br /> A stronger enemy can also be represented via an enemy organization a la Deviant. This could be beneficial if you're playing out their interference via dice roll as Monteparnas suggested as an organization might have numerous ways to be a problem for the group beyond the threat of just intense violence or some of an enemy's number can just get a pallet swap and suddenly group plans have to be adjusted because they don't want the Table knowing they're involved with this or having reasons to escalate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}